```
# <Name> Lightroom Preset — Community Recipes & Settings

<1-2 sentence intro naming the preset.>

## Reference Looks
How photographers/restorers/filmmakers recreate this era today (film emulation packs, restoration workflows, period-correct grading), with sources.

## Slider Starting Points
A table of plausible ranges per panel (Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Clarity, Texture, Dehaze, Saturation, Vibrance, HSL, Color Grading, Grain, Vignette) and how each contributes to the era look. Ranges, not invented exact "community consensus" values.

## Common Mistakes
4-6 bullets (e.g. over-graining a grain-free process, modern grade tropes that break period plausibility).

## See Also
```

## Rules
- The repo's hard rules (from AGENTS.md / STYLEGUIDE.md): presets must NOT use the Calibration panel, must not set Temperature/Tint unless defining, must keep `|Vibrance - Saturation| <= 5`, HSL Saturation within ±60, Blacks not below -30, and any preset with `GrainAmount > 0` must have `Sharpness="10"` and Clarity/Texture/Dehaze at 0 (a period look that explicitly needs Clarity/Dehaze as a defining trait may override, with a note).
- **Only edit an XMP if a defining trait is clearly contradicted by research** (e.g. a grain-free process carrying heavy grain, or an era that is high-contrast rendered flat). Record every change in the Verification section. Two known suspects: `Dust Bowl Documentary` claims atmospheric haze but has Dehaze=0; check whether that is a real miss. Do not touch names, UUIDs, Look blocks, or tone curves.
- Do NOT run git commands. Do NOT rename or move files. Do NOT modify any preset outside your 7.
- After any XMP edit, verify all 7 of your presets still parse as strict XML (python `xml.etree.ElementTree`).
- Keep prose crisp and factual. No filler. Aim for ~450-800 words per characteristics.md and ~250-450 per community-recipes.md.

